# Consumer-owned schema extension (issue 03 — workspaces, membership & tenant
# scoping). sqlgen generates a plain `createWorkspace` that inserts only the
# workspace row; it cannot also record the creator as owner. But a Workspace is
# only reachable once the caller is a Member of it (the X-Workspace-Id
# middleware rejects selecting a Workspace you do not belong to), so the very
# first Membership must be minted server-side, atomically, as the Workspace is
# created — a client can never do it in a second round-trip. `bootstrapWorkspace`
# is that operation; the generated `createWorkspace` remains as the lower-level
# primitive. The matching resolver body lives in tenancy.resolvers.go and is
# preserved across `sqlgen generate` by gqlgen's copy-through semantics.

"""
Details for founding a new Workspace. The caller (from the request's bearer
token) becomes the Workspace's `owner`; no Workspace is selected via
X-Workspace-Id for this operation.
"""
input BootstrapWorkspaceInput {
  "Human-readable workspace name"
  name: String!
  "URL-safe unique short name"
  slug: String!
}

extend type Mutation {
  """
  Create a Workspace and record the authenticated caller as its `owner` via a
  Membership, in one transaction. This is how a User founds a Workspace (PRD
  story 4); unlike the generated `createWorkspace`, it also establishes the
  ownership Membership without which the new Workspace would be unreachable.
  """
  bootstrapWorkspace(input: BootstrapWorkspaceInput!): Workspace!
}
